Ladybird mirrors each web storage area in the process that reads it:
one synchronous connect loads the map and registers the process for
every later change, reads answer from the mirror, and a write is
checked against the quota with the browser's rule, applied to the
mirror, and sent one-way. The browser's jar relays every change as a
key-level delta to the other connected processes, acknowledges a write
to its writer, and answers a write the quota refused with the bottle's
contents, which the mirror resets to before re-applying what it still
has in flight. A delta for a key with an unacknowledged local write,
or any delta while a local clear is unacknowledged, is not applied: the
browser serialized it before the pending write.

Blink does the same. Its CachedStorageArea holds a StorageAreaMap,
primes it with the synchronous StorageArea::GetAll(new_observer) call,
sends Put, Delete and DeleteAll with a StorageAreaSource, and receives
KeyChanged, KeyDeleted, AllDeleted and KeyChangeFailed on its
StorageAreaObserver; pending_mutations_by_key_ makes it skip non-local
mutations for keys with unacknowledged local writes, and
KeyChangeFailed reverts the one key. WebKit's StorageAreaMap connects
with NetworkStorageManager::ConnectToStorageAreaSync, tracks
m_pendingValueChanges the same way, sends SetItem with an async reply
whose error case carries every item for syncItems(), and the network
process's StorageAreaBase::dispatchEvents sends DispatchStorageEvent
to every listener, naming the source StorageAreaImpl on the source
connection's copy so that Storage object is skipped. Gecko's LSNG
takes a per-task LSSnapshot over PBackgroundLSSnapshot instead,
prefilled and checkpointed at a stable state and marked dirty when
another process writes, and relays cross-process events through
PBackgroundLSObserver::Observe; its session storage is a content-side
SessionStorageCache checkpointed to the parent at a stable state.

Ladybird fires the in-process storage events at write time, as the
spec's broadcast and Gecko do; Blink and WebKit wait for the browser's
echo. It drops every delta while a local clear is unacknowledged, where
Blink and WebKit apply such deltas and can leave one key out of step
until the next change. And on a refused write it reports the revert to
the window that wrote as well; Blink's KeyChangeFailed skips the source
window. A window that starts listening for storage events gets its
storage connected, as Blink's DOMWindowStorageController does when a
storage listener is added.
